  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 207 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA drier start to kick off the long term period (Saturday) with \nhigh pressure over the Deep South and weak W/SW flow at the mid-\nlevels. Most of the action will be occurring well to our north as \nwaves of mid-level energy round a broad longwave trough. \nTemperatures rebound fairly quickly Saturday into Sunday as a \nsystem developing over the Plains turns winds to the S/SSW. \nForecast highs Saturday will be in the 50s to low 60s with more \nwidespread temperatures in the 60s (and perhaps a few places \nnearing 70) by Sunday. With temperatures warming, we'll also see \nan increase in moisture and the return of rain showers beginning \nas early as Sunday afternoon across parts of North GA spreading \neast through the day. The only real uncertainty that remains is \nin regards to how much rainfall we receive from Sunday's system. \nThere's still a notable spread among ensembles and deterministic \nguidance on total rainfall and it's a little too soon to hone in \non specific amounts. Any rainfall that we do receive will be much \nappreciated so stay tuned. Rain showers may linger across parts of\nCentral GA early Monday morning as the system departs but high \npressure will begin to take up residence across the eastern US. \n\nBetween late Monday to Wednesday-ish high pressure stays in place\nwith a few impulses passing through very weak SW flow aloft. \nSunday's system does not look particularly strong thus \ntemperatures remain steady -- highs in the 50s and 60s each day --\nwith mostly dry conditions expected.",
